WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.830
Over the course of roughly two dozen games,
the hero of Hyrule has fought the evil, pig-faced

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:11.179
Ganon, rescued various princesses, and kept
the mysterious Triforce out of enemy hands.

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:15.079
That's all well and good... it's just a shame
that Link causes so much trouble along the

00:00:15.079 --> 00:00:16.079
way.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.130
It's really not okay to break every pot you
see, dude.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:23.550
Whether skyward-bound, adrift in time, or
steeped in Twilight, the various incarnations

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.810
of Link have saved Hyrule countless times
over the Legend of Zelda series.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.839
Unfortunately, that doesn't mean the beloved
hero hasn't done his fair share of creepy

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:33.300
stuff along the way.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.829
What kind of stuff?

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:35.829
Well…

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:36.829
Ghost creep

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:41.539
Given that he doesn't say much, it's easy
to assume that Link is a humble, selfless

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:42.539
hero.

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:46.511
But according to Hyrule Historia, the official
guide to the Legend of Zelda series, that's

00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:48.210
not entirely true.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:52.960
After the events of the Ocarina of Time and
Majora's Mask, our hero is so furious that

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.530
he's not remembered for his accomplishments
that he sticks around for hundreds of years

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.219
as a ghost, unable to let go.

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:04.199
In Twilight Princess, he actually haunts the
younger Link, one of the Hero of Time's many

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:05.199
descendants.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.820
And it's not just like he takes the form of
a grumpy old man: he stalks Link Jr. as a

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.530
glowing wolf and terrifying skeleton called
Hero's Shade.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.310
It actually ends up working out pretty well
for young Link, since the skeleton teaches

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.470
Link advanced sword-fighting techniques.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:25.180
But introducing yourself via wolf attack and
a one-punch knockdown just to prove you're

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:29.210
hot stuff is unbecoming of any Link, living
or dead.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.050
Chicken smacking

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:35.080
When wandering poultry first appeared in A
Link to the Past, they didn't do much but

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.650
hop around and cluck, leaving players to wonder
what exactly they were good for.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:43.880
The answer is, well, pretty much nothing,
unless you like whacking farm animals with

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.140
a sword.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:50.120
Hyrule's poultry have been hit so often that
they've had to organize to stay safe.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:54.550
Smack a cucco too many times and it'll summon
its friends, who swarm Link from all directions

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.020
and peck him to death.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.490
This isn't a one-time thing, either.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.860
Attackable and vengeful cuccos have appeared
in almost every Zelda game from A Link to

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.690
the Past all the way through Breath of the
Wild.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.540
So, this begs the question: who's worse?

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:13.930
The guy who programs in birds you can injure,
or he player who actively injures birds?

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:17.769
Some things never change, and Link abusing
animals is one of them.

00:02:17.769 --> 00:02:19.239
Grave robbing

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:24.690
In Hyrule, goodies are stashed everywhere:
in pots, under bridges, in chests, and in

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.680
the guts of every monster you kill.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.719
And just like in the real world, some of Hyrule's
treasures are buried with their owners.

00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:35.299
But when Link's on a quest, not even basic
morality will stand between him and looting

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:38.920
some corpses.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.519
In the original Legend of Zelda's second quest,
Link uncovers a dungeon entrance by pushing

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:44.989
aside a gravestone.

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:49.870
In A Link to the Past, knocking a gravestone
and climbing into the tomb below nets Link

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:51.959
a cape with invisibility powers.

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:56.219
Look, if great grandpa wants to be buried
with his invisibility cape, who's going to

00:02:56.219 --> 00:02:57.310
say no?

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.989
Link's worst offense, however, occurs in Ocarina
of Time, where the young adventurer doesn't

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:04.840
even bother to do the grave-robbing himself.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:10.079
When Link visits Kakariko Graveyard, he can
fork over 10 rupees to make the local gravekeeper

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:15.069
dig a hole wherever Link wants, uncovering
anything from a few bucks to an entire Heart

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.069
Container.

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:20.139
Even if you call it "Dampé's Heart-Pounding
Gravedigging Tour," it's still ghoulish.

00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:24.400
You may find a treasure, but you may also
find Aunt Bertha's jawbone and a lifetime

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.400
of nightmares.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.889
Fortunately for Link's treasure horde, respect
for the dead isn't an issue.

00:03:29.889 --> 00:03:32.430
He destroyed an island… for nothing

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:37.249
In Link's Awakening, our hero Link washes
up on Koholint Island after his ship is wrecked

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:38.769
during a storm.

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:43.180
Naturally, Link begins looking for a way home
by killing everything in sight, and learns

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.650
that he can escape Koholint by waking up the
Wind Fish, a slumbering monster at the heart

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:48.720
of the island.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.609
But there's a catch: Koholint is the product
of the Wind Fish's dream, and waking him will

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:57.469
destroy the island and all of its inhabitants.

00:03:57.469 --> 00:03:59.099
That's exactly what Link does.

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:04.069
The island and all of its inhabitants valish,
and Link wakes up in the middle of the ocean,

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:05.629
clinging to a piece of driftwood.

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:07.010
This isn't just fan-speculation, either.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.889
The Hyrule Historia calls his choice to destroy
Koholint a "bitter decision."

00:04:11.889 --> 00:04:16.760
All that, and Link doesn't even get back to
Hyrule; that particular version of Link sets

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:21.360
sail after his adventure, but "his further
whereabouts are unknown," meaning that Link

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.790
effectively destroyed an island for absolutely
no reason.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.240
Harassing Zelda

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:32.920
While video game Link is almost entirely non-verbal,
cartoon link was an entirely different kind

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:33.920
of hero.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.850
There's no good explanation for why the writers
of the 1989 Legend of Zelda animated series

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:44.350
turned Link from noble hero into a wise-cracking
brat who's constantly saved by the princess.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:49.030
If cartoon Link had just been a whiner, it
would be bad enough, but he was also pretty

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:51.600
clearly a sexual predator on top of everything
else.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:58.450
From the very first episode, he ogles Zelda
while she's getting dressed, catcalls her…

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.500
"Lookin' good, Princess.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.030
Especially from this angle!"

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.840
....and tries to kiss Zelda when she isn't
expecting it.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.870
That all happens in the first six minutes,
and it doesn't get better.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:16.320
Over the course of the show's 13-episode run,
Link spends almost all of his time trying

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.010
to score, whether it's with Zelda, or with
weird, anonymous witches.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.310
"Dying… kiss me!"

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:25.410
"Ugh!"

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:29.750
Combine Link's unseemly behavior with his
general whiny-ness and you're left hoping

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:31.390
that Ganon wins.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:33.960
Speaking of Link's weird perversions...

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:35.860
The Hyrule Streaker

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.640
Link's unusual behavior didn't end in the
'80s.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:44.390
In Breath of the Wild, Link roams the free-form
open-world game with very few limits.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:49.170
Want to ignore the main quest and just spend
your time causing havoc among Hyrule's ruins?

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:50.170
Go right ahead.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:53.210
Want to track down shrines and defeat Ganon
in your undies?

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:54.780
You can do that, too.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:59.500
Basically, the game is so free-form that Link
doesn't have to wear any other clothes if

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.590
you don't want him to, and that's more than
enough to offend Hylians' delicate sensibilities.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.520
While some people don't mind seeing Link's
tighty whiteys, a teenage girl who lives in

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:13.860
Kakariko Village is absolutely traumatized
by the sight of Link's barely-contained buns.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:18.340
At best, making people uncomfortable like
that is selfish and only profitable if you

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:19.440
live in New York City.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.100
At worst, it's a crime, and one that should
be punished.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.290
Preferably with death by chickens.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:26.450
Thanks for watching!

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:28.980
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.090
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

